/// \defgroup grp_w2_ini_template_refs Reference, duration and font field parsers
/// \ingroup grp_ini
/// \brief INI field parsers that resolve a name to another definition, turn
///        milliseconds into logic frames, or read a font description.
///
/// These are the parse functions behind many FieldParse rows: the
/// INI statics that look up a weapon, upgrade, FX list, object creation
/// list, particle system or mapped image by name, the two integer duration
/// parsers, and GlobalLanguage::parseFontDesc. The INI class page explains
/// the field parser signature they share and compares how each reference
/// parser treats a name it cannot find. \ref sub_ini is the subsystem page
/// for the load as a whole.

/// \class INI
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ini_template_refs
/// \brief The game-data reader: reads one INI file a line at a time and
///        hands each block and field to a parse function.
///
/// INI is how almost every definition in the game reaches C++: objects and
/// their modules, weapons, FX lists, upgrades, particle systems, mapped
/// images, fonts and many global settings. A block keyword at the start of a
/// line selects a block parser, and the block parser walks a FieldParse
/// table that maps each field name to a field parser and a place in the
/// object being filled. \ref sub_ini is the subsystem page for the load
/// as a whole.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// An INI object holds the state of one load, including the file being
/// read, the current line and line number, the separator sets, the
/// block-end and end-of-script tokens, and the first line of the current
/// block, which error messages quote. Zero Hour keeps that last buffer only
/// in debug and internal builds; BFME 2's retail reader has it. BFME 2's
/// reader also has further members whose roles are not yet established.
/// It is short-lived, normally a local in the function that loads
/// a file or a directory. Zero Hour declares the copy constructor and the
/// assignment operator private, so a reader cannot be copied. Everything a
/// load produces goes into stores that other subsystems own, such as
/// TheWeaponStore, TheFXListStore and TheUpgradeCenter.
///
/// \par Parse functions
/// Most of the class is static parse functions of two shapes:
/// - Block parsers take only the reader. They are registered under a block
///   keyword, and each finds or creates its object and fills it.
/// - Field parsers take (ini, instance, store, userData). ini is the reader,
///   positioned just after the field name; instance is the object being
///   filled; store is the address of the field: instance plus the row's
///   offset, plus the extra offset of its table when the table was added
///   to a multi-table parse with one (INI::initFromINIMulti); userData is
///   that row's extra pointer, for example a list of names. A catch-all
///   row, which has no field name and ends a table, receives the field name
///   itself as userData. A field parser reads its own
///   value tokens, usually with INI::getNextToken, and writes the result
///   through store.
///
/// \par Fields that name another definition
/// Several field parsers turn a name into a pointer while the field is read:
/// INI::parseWeaponTemplate, INI::parseUpgradeTemplate, INI::parseFXList,
/// INI::parseObjectCreationList, INI::parseParticleSystemTemplate and
/// INI::parseMappedImage. Each stores only the pointer, never the name, so
/// nothing in them can resolve the name later. What happens to a name that
/// does not resolve differs between them:
/// - FXList: the load stops with an error, unless the name is None.
/// - Weapon, object creation list, upgrade, particle system and mapped
///   image: the field is set to null and nothing is reported.
/// - Upgrade and mapped image: when their store does not exist yet, the
///   field is left as it was.
///
/// \par Duration fields
/// INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt and INI::parseDurationUnsignedShort read
/// milliseconds and store logic frames, rounded up; INI::parseDurationReal
/// stores the unrounded frame count. The data therefore states times in
/// real time while the simulation counts frames (\ref page_frame_loop).
///
/// \par Modding
/// - A referenced definition has to be loaded before the file that
///   references it, because the lookup happens as the field is read. Which
///   files load first is decided by the order in which subsystems are
///   created and by the subsystem legend, SubsystemLegend.ini, which lists
///   the INI files each subsystem loads; a few files are fixed paths
///   instead (\ref sub_engine_core).
/// - Except for FX lists, a misspelt reference is silent: the field ends up
///   null, as if None had been written. Weapon, FX list, object creation
///   list and upgrade names go through NameKeyGenerator::nameToKey, which
///   compares names case-sensitively, so the case must match the
///   definition too.
/// - Name tokens are split at spaces, tabs and the equals sign, so a
///   referenced name cannot contain them.
/// - Durations are written in milliseconds and take effect in whole logic
///   frames; see INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - INI::loadFile sets the floating-point mode with setFPMode before it
///   parses (single precision, round to nearest), so converted values such
///   as durations are computed in that mode.
/// - The parsers store raw pointers into the stores, which therefore must
///   not move or free a definition while anything still points to it.
///
/// \note WWLib's INIClass is a separate, Westwood-style INI reader with its
///       own sections and entries; it is not this class.
/// \see INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, INI::parseWeaponTemplate,
///      INI::parseFXList, GlobalLanguage::parseFontDesc

/// \fn static void INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: reads a duration in milliseconds and stores it as a
///        whole number of logic frames, rounded up.
///
/// FieldParse tables use it for integer time fields, so INI data gives
/// those times in milliseconds while the code that reads the field counts
/// logic frames.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The value is the next token, read with INI::getNextToken and converted
/// with INI::scanUnsignedInt, so a macro name or a math expression that
/// starts with \# (such as \#ADD) is accepted. Otherwise the number is read
/// with sscanf's \%u, and the load stops with an INIException only when the
/// token, after macro expansion, does not begin with a number. Trailing
/// text after the digits is ignored, so 250ms reads as 250, and a leading
/// minus sign is accepted and wraps to a very large value. It is
/// multiplied by the engine's frames-per-millisecond factor and rounded up
/// with ceil. In the current reconstruction that factor is a
/// global whose retail value is 0.005, which matches the 5 logic frames per
/// second described on \ref page_frame_loop; only reads of the global have
/// been found. Zero Hour computes the same thing with
/// ConvertDurationFromMsecsToFrames at 30 frames per second.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The object being filled. Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The unsigned int field that receives the frame
///                 count.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// At 5 frames per second every duration becomes a multiple of 200
/// milliseconds: 0 stays 0, 1 to 200 become one frame, 201 to 400 two
/// frames, and so on. A value of 250 therefore behaves like 400. A negative
/// value is not rejected: it wraps to a very large unsigned number, which
/// gives an extremely long duration.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The frames-per-millisecond factor is a separate value from the logic
/// rate (see \ref page_frame_loop). A port that changes the logic rate must
/// change this factor to match, or every INI duration is still converted at
/// 5 frames per second. Even when both change together, the rounding step
/// changes, and saves and replays recorded at the old rate no longer match
/// the simulation.
/// \see INI::parseDurationUnsignedShort, INI::parseDurationReal,
///      INI::scanUnsignedInt

/// \fn static void INI::parseDurationUnsignedShort(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: like INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, but stores the
///        frame count in a 16-bit unsigned field.
///
/// The conversion is the same: milliseconds read through
/// INI::scanUnsignedInt, multiplied by the frames-per-millisecond factor and
/// rounded up. Only the store differs.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The object being filled. Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The 16-bit unsigned field that receives the frame
///                 count.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \warning There is no range check. A frame count above 65,535 keeps only
///          its low 16 bits, so a very long duration wraps round to a short
///          one without any error. Zero Hour narrows the value the same way.
///          BFME 2 also has a byte-sized variant, not yet named, that does
///          reject more than 255 frames with an error.
/// \see INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt

/// \fn static void INI::parseWeaponTemplate(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: looks a weapon name up in TheWeaponStore and stores
///        the WeaponTemplate pointer, null included.
///
/// Used by fields that name a weapon, such as CollideWeapon. The pointer is
/// resolved once, while the field is read.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads one token with INI::getNextToken and passes it to
/// WeaponStore::findWeaponTemplate. That returns null for None, compared
/// without regard to case, and otherwise looks the weapon up by its name
/// key from NameKeyGenerator::nameToKey. The result is stored even when it
/// is null. TheWeaponStore must already exist; it is not checked.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The object being filled. Not used.
/// \param store    [out] A pointer-to-WeaponTemplate field.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour only asserts on an unknown weapon in debug builds, so a
///       Zero Hour release build behaves like BFME 2: an unknown name is not
///       an error.
///
/// \par Modding
/// A misspelt weapon name, or one whose case differs from the definition,
/// leaves the field null without any message. The weapon must be defined in
/// a file that loads before this one.
/// \see WeaponStore::findWeaponTemplate, INI::parseFXList

/// \fn static void INI::parseUpgradeTemplate(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: looks an upgrade name up in TheUpgradeCenter and
///        stores the UpgradeTemplate pointer.
///
/// Used by fields that name an upgrade, such as Upgrade.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads one token with INI::getNextToken. When TheUpgradeCenter exists it
/// calls UpgradeCenter::findUpgrade, which turns the name into a name key
/// with NameKeyGenerator::nameToKey and looks the key up, and stores the
/// result, null included. When TheUpgradeCenter does not exist the token is
/// consumed and the field keeps whatever value it had.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The object being filled. Not used.
/// \param store    [out] A pointer-to-UpgradeTemplate field.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour throws an error when TheUpgradeCenter does not exist yet;
///       BFME 2, like BFME 1, silently skips the field. UpgradeCenter::findUpgrade
///       has no special case for None, so None gives null only because no
///       upgrade has that name.
///
/// \par Modding
/// An unknown upgrade name leaves the field null without any message, and
/// the case of the name must match the definition.
/// \see UpgradeCenter::findUpgrade

/// \fn static void INI::parseFXList(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: looks an FX list name up in TheFXListStore and
///        stores the FXList pointer; an unknown name stops the load.
///
/// Used by fields that name an FX list, such as ToppleFX and FireFlankFX.
/// It is the only reference parser on this page that treats a missing
/// definition as an error.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads one token with INI::getNextToken and calls
/// FXListStore::findFXList, which returns null for None (compared without
/// regard to case) and otherwise looks the list up by its name key. If the
/// result is null and the token is not None, it throws an INIException
/// with the message "iniParseFXList -- FXList %s not found! Either add the
/// FXList or remove the reference to it." Otherwise the result, null for
/// None, is stored.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The object being filled. Not used.
/// \param store    [out] A pointer-to-FXList field.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour only asserts in debug builds and stores null. The error
///       is in BFME 1 as well.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Write None to leave an FX field empty. Any other name must match a
/// defined FX list exactly, and that list must load before the file that
/// uses it, or the game stops loading with the error above.
/// \see FXListStore::findFXList, INI::parseWeaponTemplate

/// \fn static void INI::parseObjectCreationList(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: looks an object creation list (OCL) name up in
///        TheObjectCreationListStore and stores the pointer, null included.
///
/// Used by fields that name an object creation list, such as CreationList.
/// ObjectCreationListStore::findObjectCreationList returns null for None
/// and otherwise looks the list up by its name key; whatever it returns is
/// stored. This is Zero Hour's parser without its debug-build assertion, so
/// an unknown name is not an error.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The object being filled. Not used.
/// \param store    [out] A pointer-to-ObjectCreationList field.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// A misspelt list name leaves the field null without any message, and the
/// list must be defined in a file that loads earlier.
/// \see ObjectCreationListStore::findObjectCreationList

/// \fn static void INI::parseParticleSystemTemplate(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: looks a particle system name up in
///        TheParticleSystemManager and stores the template pointer, or null.
///
/// Used by fields that name a particle system, such as
/// DisableFXParticleSystem.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads one token with INI::getNextToken and calls
/// ParticleSystemManager::findTemplate. The template is stored only when
/// one was found and the token is not None (compared without regard to
/// case); in every other case the field is set to null. The manager must
/// already exist; it is not checked.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The object being filled. Not used.
/// \param store    [out] A pointer-to-ParticleSystemTemplate field.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \note Zero Hour stores whatever the lookup returns, so there a template
///       actually named None could be referenced. BFME 2, like BFME 1,
///       always maps None to null.
///
/// \par Modding
/// An unknown particle system name leaves the field null without any
/// message.
/// \see ParticleSystemManager::findTemplate

/// \fn static void INI::parseMappedImage(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: looks a mapped image name up in
///        TheMappedImageCollection and stores the Image pointer.
///
/// Used by fields that name a UI image, such as ImageName. In Zero Hour a
/// mapped image is a named rectangle of a texture file, defined in a
/// MappedImage block (INI::parseMappedImageDefinition).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Reads one token with INI::getNextToken. When TheMappedImageCollection
/// exists it stores the result of ImageCollection::findImageByName, null for
/// an unknown name. When the collection does not exist the token is
/// consumed and the field is left as it was. A comment in Zero Hour's
/// source explains why: WorldBuilder parses command buttons for their
/// information but never uses their images, while in the game and GUIEdit
/// the collection always exists. The body is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The object being filled. Not used.
/// \param store    [out] A pointer-to-Image field.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// An unknown image name gives a null image without any message. The image
/// has to be defined in a MappedImage block that loads before the
/// referencing file.
/// \see ImageCollection::findImageByName

/// \class GlobalLanguage
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ini_template_refs
/// \brief Language-dependent display settings, chiefly the fonts the
///        interface uses, filled from an INI Language block.
///
/// GlobalLanguage is an engine subsystem with a single instance,
/// TheGlobalLanguageData, which GameEngine::init creates through the
/// engine's initSubsystem helper. The Language block parser,
/// INI::parseLanguageDefinition, fills that instance through the class's
/// field table, and does nothing when the instance does not exist. Zero
/// Hour and BFME 1 read the block from a Language.ini in the folder of the
/// installed language; BFME 2's own loading code is not reconstructed yet.
///
/// \par What it holds
/// Zero Hour's fields are the Unicode font name, a list of local font files,
/// the military caption speed and delay, a hard word wrap flag, a
/// resolution font adjustment and a FontDesc for each text role, from
/// CopyrightFont and MessageFont to the credits fonts. Open-BFME-1's table
/// for BFME 1 adds decimal, thousand and minute-to-second separators, an
/// audio language, an audio subtitle font and help box fonts, and drops
/// Zero Hour's MilitaryCaptionDelayMS. BFME 2's field table is not
/// reconstructed yet, so its exact field list is not established.
///
/// \par Users in BFME 2
/// - InGameUI::init copies several of these font settings into its own
///   fields.
/// - W3DDisplayString::notifyTextChanged reads the hard word wrap flag.
/// - GlobalLanguage::adjustFontSize scales a point size by the screen width
///   divided by 1024 and rounds down. SuperweaponInfo::setFont is one
///   caller.
/// - GlobalLanguage::onGameEngineExit removes the private font resources
///   registered from local font files and deletes the temporary font copies
///   (files whose names start with lrf) from a folder under the system temp
///   path. In Open-BFME-1's reconstruction of BFME 1, the init reads each
///   local font through the game's file system, so it may come from an
///   archive, writes it to such a temporary file and registers that file as
///   a private font. BFME 2's cleanup matches that scheme.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's adjustFontSize divides by 800, blends the result with
///       the resolution font adjustment and keeps it between 1 and 2
///       times the original size. BFME 2's has no blend and no limits.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fonts, sizes and boldness are data: change them in the Language block
/// (see GlobalLanguage::parseFontDesc for the format). The set of font
/// fields is fixed by the field table in the executable.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Font sizes grow linearly with the horizontal resolution, without an
///   upper limit, and ignore the height and aspect ratio. A 2048-pixel-wide
///   screen doubles them.
/// - Local fonts go through Win32 GDI private font calls and temporary
///   files (BFME 2 removes them with RemoveFontResourceExA), which a port
///   has to replace.
/// \see GlobalLanguage::parseFontDesc, INI::parseLanguageDefinition

/// \fn static void GlobalLanguage::parseFontDesc(INI *ini, void *instance, void *store, const void *userData)
/// \brief Field parser: reads a font description (name, point size and bold
///        flag) into a FontDesc.
///
/// The Language block's font fields use it, and BFME 2 also calls it
/// directly from a parser, not yet named, that reads a DefaultMessageFont
/// field into a temporary FontDesc and copies that into its own object.
/// The body is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Three values are read from the rest of the line, in order and without
/// labels:
/// - the name, through INI::getNextQuotedAsciiString: one word, or a
///   double-quoted string that may contain spaces.
/// - the point size, through INI::scanInt on the next token.
/// - the bold flag, through INI::scanBool: Yes or No in any case; anything
///   else stops the load with an error.
///
/// A line that ends early also stops the load, because the size and bold
/// flag are read with INI::getNextToken, which requires a token. FontDesc's
/// default constructor, matched in BFME 2, sets Arial Unicode MS, size 12,
/// not bold.
///
/// \param ini      The reader, positioned after the field name.
/// \param instance The object being filled. Not used.
/// \param store    [out] The FontDesc to fill.
/// \param userData Not used.
///
/// \par Modding
/// A font line looks like this (the field name is only an example):
/// \code
/// MessageFont = "Arial Unicode MS" 12 No
/// \endcode
/// The size is a point size before any scaling; callers that scale it use
/// GlobalLanguage::adjustFontSize.
/// \see GlobalLanguage, INI::getNextQuotedAsciiString, INI::scanBool
